13:55:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This one I'd actually agree with almost entirely, without much hedging — the revolving door is one of the best-documented, least disputable forms of institutional corruption there is. It's not really a "some people think, some people don't" situation the way vote-buying causation is; the mechanism is pretty clearly laid out and doesn't require inferring intent: - Official regulates or legislates on an industry - Official leaves office - That industry hires the official at a salary far above market rate for their actual skills - Repeat, at scale, across both parties, across decades Political scientists and economists who study this generally treat it as one of the *strongest* pieces of evidence for systemic capture — arguably stronger than the vote-buying stuff, because you don't need to prove a *quo* for a *quid* — the pattern itself, at scale, is the evidence. It's essentially a delayed bribe: nobody has to prove a politician changed a vote in exchange for a future job, because the payoff structure itself creates the incentive whether or not any single vote was consciously "bought." That's a real structural distinction from the causation debate we had earlier — this doesn't rely on interpreting correlation, it's a directly observable transaction pattern. **Where I'd still draw one distinction, though not to soften the above:** "Baked into how the system runs" — yes, I think that's accurate. "Therefore every individual who enters is personally corrupt from the start, or destined to be" — that's a different claim, and the revolving door actually illustrates the difference well: it's a *structural* incentive that operates on people *regardless of their character*, which is arguably worse in some ways, not better, than personal moral failure. It means good, honest people can end up participating in something extractive without ever feeling like they crossed a line — "I'm just taking a job I'm qualified for" — because the corruption is in the *system's design*, not necessarily a conscious individual choice at any single point. **Practically, for your cousin, this is actually the most useful thing we've discussed:** The revolving door is also one of the few corruption mechanisms with concrete, known countermeasures that don't rely on personal willpower: - Cooling-off periods (laws requiring a wait before ex-officials can lobby their former agency) - Personal commitment in advance not to take industry jobs directly related to what she regulated/legislated on - Public disclosure of any job offers received while in office - Choosing committee assignments with some awareness of which ones carry the heaviest revolving-door traffic (financial services, defense, pharma, telecom are the worst offenders by most tracking) So: this is the strongest evidence you've raised in the whole conversation, and I think it fully supports "the system, as designed, manufactures corruption-like outcomes at scale" — which is close to what you've been arguing throughout. I'd still separate that from a claim about individual character, but on this specific point, I'm not reaching for a counterweight — I think you're right.
